    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5438b733_b93bb279",
        "filename": "service_vm/requests/src/dice.rs",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 134,
      "author": {
        "id": 1958315
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-11-29T14:23:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Implementing this function requires support for all the key types (PubKeyEd25519 / PubKeyECDSA256 / PubKeyECDSA384), for both cryptographic algorithms and CoseKey serialization. It will take some time (only PubKeyECDSA256 is supported for now). \n\nWhat about just removing the empty function in question for now and reintroduce it when the infrastructure is ready to accommodate all key types? Like this, I can continue completing the certificate extension with the information extracted from the DICE chain and adding support for different key types in parallel.",
      "parentUuid": "4f3fb6f3_df661ab7",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 134,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 134,
        "endChar": 94
      },
      "revId": "0b7295df65e93f3373993516ad90b3ebd7bd4940",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ad2ba86d_deaf1788",
        "filename": "service_vm/requests/src/dice.rs",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 134,
      "author": {
        "id": 1060831
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-11-29T14:38:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Checking in known insecure code that will sign certs makes me nervous. (That also applies to the Microdroid checks.)\nI know this is behind a flag, but even so.\n\nCan we make sure that requesting an attestation for a pVM will always fail, until we have all the necessary checks in place? That won\u0027t break your tests, I think?",
      "parentUuid": "5438b733_b93bb279",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 134,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 134,
        "endChar": 94
      },
      "revId": "0b7295df65e93f3373993516ad90b3ebd7bd4940",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8f94687b_f40cfa28",
        "filename": "service_vm/requests/src/dice.rs",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 134,
      "author": {
        "id": 1958315
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-11-29T16:50:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Can we make sure that requesting an attestation for a pVM will always fail, until we have all the necessary checks in place? That won\u0027t break your tests, I think?\n\nUnfortunately, if we interrupt the verification midway, we will no longer be able to verify the certificate in the `rialto_test`, as the certificate is only issued if all verifications are successfully completed. Having the rialto_test is crucial because it assesses the entire request processing flow with the integration of rialto/pvmfw/vmbase, and not as flaky as the java tests. More specifically, unlike the unit tests in `service_vm_requires` or `bssl`, it provides a comprehensive coverage that allowed us to catch some low-level errors in libc/virtio-driver of vmbase such as [1] in the past.\n\nIn this case, I believe we should rely on the flag. Anyway, we will not issue any certificates until the flag is enabled.\n\n[1] https://r.android.com/2660016",
      "parentUuid": "ad2ba86d_deaf1788",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 134,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 134,
        "endChar": 94
      },
      "revId": "0b7295df65e93f3373993516ad90b3ebd7bd4940",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0721aab9_c6e1b2d3",
        "filename": "service_vm/requests/src/dice.rs",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 134,
      "author": {
        "id": 1060831
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-11-29T17:49:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I thought the working rialto_test was for a non-protected VM?",
      "parentUuid": "8f94687b_f40cfa28",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 134,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 134,
        "endChar": 94
      },
      "revId": "0b7295df65e93f3373993516ad90b3ebd7bd4940",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9f4683fd_b14279e0",
        "filename": "service_vm/requests/src/dice.rs",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 134,
      "author": {
        "id": 1958315
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-11-30T08:51:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "rialto_test works for both protected and non-protected VM. When testing a protected VM, we expect it to fail at the DICE chain comparison step, while a non-protected VM should go till the end and return a certificate that we can examine for its content.\n\nFrom an infrastructure POV, the code that runs through both types of VMs is almost identical. Both VMs are built with the vmbase(memory,vsock..)/rialto/request_processing layers. The main difference is the MMIO support and the retrieval of the DICE chain in rialto. In essence, the request processing performed by the protected rialto is extensively covered by the e2e non-protected rialto_test.\n\nBy making the attestation to fail midway, we miss out on testing the section of code that follows the failure. This can be a problem as `vmbase` is also modified by other developers, and there is a risk that something could break in this section of code.",
      "parentUuid": "0721aab9_c6e1b2d3",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 134,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 134,
        "endChar": 94
      },
      "revId": "0b7295df65e93f3373993516ad90b3ebd7bd4940",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
